WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.740
Passwords should never be stored in plaintext or reversible form.

2
00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:18.280
A Node.js application should store password verifiers using password-hashing algorithms designed for credential storage, such as Argon2id, bcrypt, or scrypt, through well-maintained libraries.

3
00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:21.900
Password hashing is different from ordinary fast hashing.

4
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:27.360
It is intentionally slower and configurable so large-scale guessing is more expensive.

5
00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:32.980
Salts should be unique per password and handled by the library or storage format correctly.

6
00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:36.940
There is an engineering tradeoff in password-hashing parameters.

7
00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:44.880
Stronger parameters increase attacker cost, but they also consume server CPU and memory during legitimate logins.

8
00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:52.020
Teams should choose settings that fit the deployment environment, monitor performance, and plan to upgrade over time.

9
00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:59.640
They should avoid custom cryptography, homegrown password transformations, or storing enough information to recover the original password.

10
00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:05.660
Verification should compare the submitted password to the stored verifier through the selected library.

11
00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:14.100
Password authentication also includes login abuse controls, account enumeration resistance, reset flows, and monitoring.

12
00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:18.680
Error messages should avoid confirming whether a specific account exists.

13
00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:29.500
Rate limiting, throttling, lockout design, bot protection, and alerts help defend login endpoints without creating unnecessary denial of service for legitimate users.

14
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:37.680
Password change and reset flows should revalidate identity and invalidate risky existing sessions or tokens where appropriate.
